  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...  \n\nPotent cold front has pushed across the area with showers and even a \nfew rumbles of thunder popping up along the international border. \nSome small hail has accompanied the more robust showers. T8s this \nmorning off the 12Z GYX RAOB were +8C and will likely fall to around \n-4C by daybreak Tuesday...remaining near the freezing mark through \nthe overnight Tuesday night. This CAA will drive temperatures into \nthe 30s across most areas tonight...though we/ll remain coupled \nwhich should prevent frost over areas that still have an active \ngrowing season.\n\nSpeaking of wind...that will be the primary focus for Tuesday with \nH9 gradient winds 30-40kts. While mixing will remain strong through \nTuesday...CAA will have ceased and there doesn/t look to be much \nisallobaric assistance to the low level winds.  So...expect surface \ngusts to reach levels a bit under the H9 winds...generally 35-40mph. \nTherefore...have not added any wind headlines to the forecast this \nafternoon.  \n\nA chilly night will be in store Tuesday night as arriving high \npressure ridge axis should allow surface winds to weaken and \ndecouple in most places. All but our most coastal sites look likely \nto see a frost...with a hard freeze likely further inland where the \ngrowing season has already ended. Blended statistical guidance with \nthe NBM which brings lows into the upper 20s in the mountains with \nlower-middle 30s elsewhere.   \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter brief shortwave ridging builds across the region Tuesday \nnight...winds aloft will back to the southwest ahead of the next \nshortwave and associated cold front Wednesday and Thursday...which \nshould pass through the region Thursday night.  Thus...expect \ntemperatures to warm quickly Wednesday and especially Thursday where \nT8s will push into the upper single digits C which...fully mixed \nwill allow highs into the 70s south and east of the mountains. \nShower chances with the cold front look largely confined to the \nmountains.  T8s fall back towards 1-3C by Friday and into the \nweekend which will result in seasonable highs in the 50s and 60s \nfrom north to south. Bottom line...no high impact weather is \nexpected across the forecast area during the Wednesday-this weekend \nperiod.",
